 "10.7:lab": "TIMELINE\n  t+ 0m  alert   online judge score below threshold; incident declared SEV-2, IC assigned\n  t+ 2m  check   provider health ok=True\n  t+ 4m  check   prompt changed 6h ago (answer@v11 -> answer@v12)\n  t+ 7m  action  moved production alias back to answer@v11\n  t+12m  verify  smoke eval 0.80 (fail, target 0.85)\n  t+14m  check   index age 74h (expected < 24h)\n  t+15m  action  enabled 'information may be out of date' banner\n  t+40m  action  restarted ingestion job; reindex complete\n  t+42m  check   guardrail block rate 1.1% (baseline 1.0%)\n  t+47m  verify  smoke eval 0.88 (pass, target 0.85)\n  t+47m  resolve service restored; monitoring for 60 minutes\n\nPOSTMORTEM (blameless)\nSummary: answer quality degraded for about 47 minutes; two contributing changes.\nImpact: grounded-answer quality fell from 0.88 to an estimated 0.77 on sampled traffic; no data exposure.\nContributing factors:\n  1. Ingestion job stalled 74h ago without alerting; answers used outdated documents.\n  2. Prompt answer@v12 shipped 6h ago; its eval set had no stale-document cases, so the drop compounded.\nWhat went well: quality alert fired; prompt rollback took 3 minutes; runbook guided the freshness check.\nAction items:\n  - Alert when index age > 24h (owner: data platform, due 1 week)\n  - Add stale-document cases to the release eval set (owner: AI team, due 1 week)\n  - Show freshness metadata in answers automatically when age > 24h (owner: product, due 2 weeks)\n  - Add the freshness check to the top of this runbook (owner: on-call lead, due now)",
 "11.1:code": "Sizing (6,000 tickets, 30 days):\n  order_status  38.6% of tickets  32.9% of agent time  median  9.0 min  reopened 6%\n  refunds       11.8% of tickets  24.7% of agent time  median 21.9 min  reopened 6%\n  how_to        26.8% of tickets  17.8% of agent time  median  7.0 min  reopened 18%\n  billing       14.7% of tickets  17.0% of agent time  median 12.1 min  reopened 5%\n  other          8.1% of tickets   7.6% of agent time  median 10.1 min  reopened 8%\n\nClaims vs data:\n  Agent, tier 1   'Half my day is looking up where an order is. It is in the ...' -> supported: 39% of tickets, 33% of agent time ('half my day' overstates it)\n  Agent, tier 1   'Refund questions take forever because I need finance to ap...' -> partly: only 12% of tickets but slow (median 22 min); bottleneck is an approval policy, not AI\n  Team lead       'The knowledge base is outdated, so agents write the same a...' -> plausible: how_to tickets reopen at 18% vs ~6% elsewhere\n\nDRAFT PROBLEM STATEMENT\nTier-1 agents spend 347 hours a month (33% of handling time) answering order-status questions\nwhose answers already exist in the ERP but are not visible in the helpdesk. Customers wait for answers they could get instantly.\nBaseline: 39% of tickets, median 9 min each, CSAT 3.9.\nTarget (90 days): halve order-status tickets via self-service and agent-side lookup; CSAT back to 4.3.\nNot in scope now: refund approvals (policy change, owner: Finance); KB refresh (phase 2 candidate).",
 "11.1:lab": "13,972 calls, 1,170 dispatcher hours in 30 days\n  route_change      33.5% of calls  46.8% of minutes\n  delivery_address  32.9% of calls  26.4% of minutes\n  app_issue         12.1% of calls   9.7% of minutes\n  pay               11.2% of calls   8.9% of minutes\n  other             10.3% of calls   8.2% of minutes\ncalls per driver-day by app version:\n  v4.1: 1.56 calls/driver-day, 13.1% about the app\n  v4.2: 1.55 calls/driver-day, 11.0% about the app\nroute changed that day: 61% of calls (route changes occur on ~25% of driver-days)\n\nFINDINGS\n1. The hypothesis is not supported: app issues are 12% of calls; v4.2 barely changes call rates.\n2. Route changes drive the load: 61% of calls happen on route-change days, and route-change calls take 47% of dispatcher minutes.\n3. Delivery-address questions are the next biggest category; worth checking address data quality.\nRECOMMENDATION\nPush route changes to drivers proactively (in-app notification with the new stop list and reason), then measure call volume on\nroute-change days for two weeks. An in-app assistant can come later for address questions if volume remains.\nNEXT TEST: A/B the proactive notification across depots before building anything conversational.",
 "11.2:code": "contract check: ok\norder: ingest -> split -> route -> reuse -> draft -> review -> assemble\ncritical path: ingest -> split -> route -> reuse -> draft -> review -> assemble (18 working days; every step waits on the previous one)\nest. cost per question: $1.01 (25% of answers need 15 min SME edits); 200-question RFP: $203\n\nfirst vertical slice: real RFPs -> ingest -> split (risk 4: question boundaries vary wildly between RFPs) -> stubbed rest -> sample output\n  risk 4  split     extract   question boundaries vary wildly between RFPs\n  risk 3  reuse     retrieval is the library clean enough?\n  risk 3  draft     generate  \n  risk 2  ingest    code      PDF and DOCX; some scanned\n  risk 2  route     classify  \n  risk 2  review    human     SMEs: 2 hours a week each\n  risk 1  assemble  code",
 "11.2:lab": "graph valid: True | order: intake -> damage -> extract -> coverage -> fraud -> triage -> summary -> decision -> payment -> letter\n  intake    code      risk 2 structured portal fields; deterministic\n  extract   extract   risk 3 police reports and estimates are varied documents; schema-bound extraction with confidence\n  coverage  query     risk 2 policy terms live in the policy system; rules are explicit\n  damage    extract   risk 5 vision model suggests severity; checked against repair estimate\n  fraud     classify  risk 3 existing fraud model plus rules; explainable features\n  triage    code      risk 2 thresholds set by claims policy, auditable\n  summary   generate  risk 2 cited summary saves adjuster reading time\n  decision  human     risk 2 HUMAN regulation: denials and large payouts need an accountable person\n  payment   query     risk 2 payment system API; deterministic\n  letter    generate  risk 2 template + generated explanation, reviewed for denials\n\nfast-track eligible (payout < $3,000, no fraud flag, clear coverage): 55% of claims, 34% of payout value\nfirst slice: intake -> extract -> coverage -> summary for adjusters (no automated decisions).\nsuccess metric: adjuster handling time per claim down 30% with extraction field accuracy >= 95% on 300 labelled claims;\nthen pilot fast-track approvals (human spot checks on 10%) for eligible claims. Damage-from-photos stays advisory until evaluated.",
 "11.3:code": "critical path using only 'likely' values: 21 days (access, policy_api, pilot, report); the simulation disagrees:\nfull scope                                   P50 26.3 d  P85 29.9 d  P(<= 27 d) = 58%\nafter cut lines (no policy_api, dashboards)  P50 24.8 d  P85 28.0 d  P(<= 27 d) = 76%\ncustomer access alone exceeds 8 days in 19% of runs: chase it in week 1, it gates everything",
 "11.3:lab": "everything: value 23, P(<=30d) 0%, P85 42.0 d\nchosen:     value 14, P(<=30d) 85%, P50 27.0 d, P85 30.0 d\nscope: ['data_access', 'clause_extract', 'eval_set', 'review_ui', 'pilot_and_report', 'analytics']\ndeferred: ['risk_scoring', 'redline_suggestions', 'sharepoint_sync']\n\nTo the sponsor: We can commit to clause extraction, the review UI and a measured pilot with analytics in six weeks,\nwith about 85% confidence; the full list has under 1% confidence. risk_scoring, redline_suggestions, sharepoint_sync move to phase 2\nand come back in if data access lands in week 1 and extraction hits target by week 3.",
